      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.07-02",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Plant varieties differ in their inherited characteristics. A high-yielding variety has been developed for greater yield potential under suitable conditions. That potential must be distinguished from the yield actually achieved. A variety capable of producing more grain with sufficient moisture and nutrients may perform poorly where those inputs are unavailable. Growing season, pest pressure and suitability to local conditions also matter. A packet label cannot replace a comparison under the farm's actual constraints.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-2.2.07-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.07-2",
        "CL-6137-2.2.07-01-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.07-01-2",
