      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.07-03",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "High-yielding varieties can increase output per hectare when their requirements are met. Higher output from existing land may reduce pressure to expand cultivation, but this is a possible consequence rather than an automatic outcome. Farmers may still expand if prices or land incentives favour it. Purchased seed and accompanying inputs may raise costs. A useful judgement compares additional output with the extra expenses, risks and resource requirements rather than treating potential yield as a guaranteed harvest.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-2.2.07-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.07-2",
        "CL-6137-2.2.07-01-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.07-01-2",
